endobj
486 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 247 0 R
/T (1. What ecological disturbance gradient did your 10 sites represent and what evidence supports that gradient?)
/E (1. What ecological disturbance gradient did your 10 sites represent and what evidence supports that gradient?)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
487 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 247 0 R
/E (The sites form a gradient from very low to high disturbance. Tubbataha Reefs Natural Park and Apo Reef Natural Park are lowest because they are remote and well protected with low fishing pressure. Danjugan Island Apo Island and Sumilon Island show moderate disturbance from tourism and small nearby populations. Olango Island and Mactan Island rank highest due to dense coastal settlement fishing and coastal development. Population density data supports this pattern. Low disturbance sites had around 400 to 463 people per unit area within a 20 km buffer while high disturbance sites such as Dumaguete and Bacong exceeded 800 people in the same buffer zone.)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
488 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 247 0 R
/E (2. How did reef topography differ among low and high disturbance sites?)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
489 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 247 0 R
/E (Low disturbance sites such as Tubbataha and Apo Reef are oceanic atoll systems with well developed slopes at 24 to 30 percent distinct reef crests and extensive lagoons covering more than 31 percent of the area. Mid disturbance sites such as Apo Island and Danjugan Island are slope dominated fringing reefs with narrow flats and little to no lagoon development. High disturbance nearshore sites such as Olango Island and Mactan Island are overwhelmingly flat dominated. Reef flats make up nearly 70 percent of the habitat with very limited slopes and almost no lagoon or reef crest features.)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
490 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 247 0 R
/E (3. Which benthic classes dominated each end of the gradient?)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
491 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 247 0 R
/E (Across all sites combined coral and algae cover averaged 26.83 percent rubble made up 23.60 percent rock was 20.48 percent sand 17.72 percent seagrass 10.13 percent and microalgal mats 1.24 percent. Low disturbance sites tended to have higher coral and algae cover and greater structural complexity. High disturbance sites were dominated by reef flats rubble and sand with proportionally less living coral and more exposed or unstable substrates.)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
492 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 247 0 R
/E (4. How did turbidity and nearby human population density vary among sites?)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
493 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 247 0 R
/E (Turbidity was not measured directly but nearshore sites with high human density such as Olango Island and Mactan Island are expected to have higher turbidity from runoff sedimentation and construction. Population density showed a clear difference. Low disturbance sites had low surrounding densities ranging from 396 to 463 people in the 20 km buffer. High disturbance sites such as Dumaguete Bacong and Sibulan recorded 799 to 861 people in the same buffer zone. This reflects intense coastal settlement and associated pressures.)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
494 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 248 0 R
/T (Questions )
/E (Questions )
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
495 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 248 0 R
/E (5. How many GBIF records and species were obtained at each site?)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
496 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 248 0 R
/E (Tubbataha Reefs Natural Park had 41 GBIF records and 12 observed species. Apo Reef Natural Park had 4 GBIF records and 4 observed species. Danjugan Island had 4 GBIF records and 3 observed species. Apo Island had 30 GBIF records and 20 observed species. Sumilon Island had 3 GBIF records and 3 observed species. Moalboal and Pescador Island area had 25 GBIF records and 17 observed species. Siquijor Island reef area had 5 GBIF records and 4 observed species. Balicasag Island had 12 GBIF records and 7 observed species. Olango Island reef area had 15 GBIF records and 9 observed species. Mactan Island reef area also had 15 GBIF records and 9 observed species.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
497 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 248 0 R
/E (6. Were sites with high species richness also sites with high GBIF sampling effort?)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
498 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 248 0 R
/E (Sites with higher GBIF sampling effort generally had higher observed species richness though the relationship was not perfectly consistent. Tubbataha Reefs Natural Park had the most records and relatively high species richness while Apo Island had the second highest number of records and the highest species richness overall. Moalboal and Pescador Island area also followed this pattern with many records and high species count. However some sites such as Apo Reef and Danjugan Island had very few records and correspondingly low species numbers which likely reflects limited sampling rather than true absence of species. Overall the data suggest that greater sampling effort tends to yield more observed species but differences in local habitat conditions may also influence the number of species recorded at each site.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
499 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 248 0 R
/E (7. Which environmental variable showed the clearest relationship with species richness for your taxonomic group?)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
500 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 248 0 R
/E (No variable showed a statistically significant relationship. Rubble cover had a weak positive association with a Spearman rho value of 0.202. Disturbance rank had a very weak positive association at 0.080. Reef extent area showed a negligible positive association at 0.055. Coral and algae cover had a weak negative association at negative 0.294. All p values were well above 0.05 so none provided clear evidence of a meaningful link.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
501 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 249 0 R
/T (Questions )
/E (Questions )
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
502 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 249 0 R
/E (8. Did your simple GLM or regression support the group's ecological hypothesis? Describe direction and uncertainty not just the p value.)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
503 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 249 0 R
/E (The hypothesis that greater disturbance or lower coral cover reduces species richness was not supported. Disturbance rank showed a slight positive trend meaning richness tended to increase marginally with higher disturbance. This trend was extremely weak and uncertain with wide scatter across sites. Coral and algae cover trended in the expected negative direction but the relationship was loose and inconsistent. Reef extent showed almost no directional trend at all. All relationships were highly uncertain with no consistent pattern emerging across the ten sites.)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
504 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 249 0 R
/E (9. What is one plausible biological explanation for the pattern?)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
505 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 249 0 R
/E (Coral species richness may depend more on local habitat complexity historical connectivity and consistent protection than on broad disturbance or cover measurements. Apo Island had moderate disturbance levels yet the highest richness likely due to long term community led management. This management maintains stable and diverse coral communities despite nearby human activity. Well enforced local protection can sustain richness even where regional pressure is moderate and this masks simple gradient based patterns.)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
506 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 249 0 R
/E (10. What are at least three reasons the pattern might be biased or confounded?)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
507 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 249 0 R
/E (First richness data came from unevenly surveyed sites so apparent differences may reflect how often an area was checked rather than true biological variation. Second disturbance correlates with island type distance from shore and larval connectivity. These natural biogeographic differences could drive richness independently of human impact. Third the study included only ten sites with narrow ranges in some variables. This makes it difficult to detect real relationships or to clearly separate human effects from natural environmental variation.)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
